title: LSP Diagnostics

The editor marks links and transclusions whose target does not exist, calls whose name nothing in the wiki defines, and filter steps that name no operator, through `textDocument/publishDiagnostics`. A misspelt name gets a quick fix. Try it in [[LSP Diagnostics - (Example)]].

!! When diagnostics are recomputed

Not on every keystroke. Diagnostics are recomputed once typing pauses for about a third of a second, so a link you are halfway through writing is not squiggled while you write it. Opening a document reports immediately; closing one clears its diagnostics and cancels any pass still waiting, unless undefined calls were listed (see below).

!! Links: what is not flagged

The links checked are the ones ~TiddlyWiki's parser finds: `[[X]]`, `[[caption|X]]`, `<$link to="X">`, `{{X}}` and `{{X||template}}`, also inside definition bodies. So a link is left alone when it is an external address, inside any filter (a filtered transclusion, a filter attribute, an `<%if%>` condition or a `\function` body, where `[[X]]` names a title rather than linking to it), inside inline code or a fenced block, inside a macro argument or another attribute value, or in the field header of a `.tid` file, where a `tags` field routinely names tiddlers that do not exist yet.

A target assembled at render time from a variable cannot be checked at all, so it is never reported.

!! Names nothing defines

A call whose name nothing in reach supplies (a definition, a parameter or a widget variable), and that nothing else in the wiki defines or sets, gets a //hint//: three grey dots under the start of the name, and a message when you hover them. ~TiddlyWiki renders a misspelt name as nothing, so this is often the only sign of a typo. Every form of call is checked: `<<name>>`, `<$transclude $variable>`, `<$macrocall $name>`, `[<name>]`, `function[name]`, a dotted operator such as `[my.fn[]]`, and a widget tag such as `<$lisst>`.

A variable can be set while the wiki renders, out of sight of anyone reading the text, so a name counts as known when:

* any tiddler defines it, declares it as a parameter, or sets it with a widget such as `<$let>`, `<$set>` or the `variable` of `<$list>`
* a shadow tiddler calls it: the core and plugins call what their ~JavaScript sets
* ~JavaScript sets it: a name it hands to action strings, as `<$radio>` does with `actionValue`, or one it builds from a prefix, as the event and message catchers do with `dom-class` or `event-param`
* it is an attribute of `<$action-sendmessage>`, which messages such as `tm-modal` pass on as variables
* it is set in wikitext held in a string, such as the `src` of an example macro

Core variables such as `currentTiddler`, and `tv-` settings, are never flagged. The price: a typo equal to a name set somewhere in the wiki is not flagged, nor is a name starting with a prefix ~JavaScript builds names from, such as `event-`, unless it is a typing slip away from a known name, as `<<list-linkz>>` is.

!! Filter steps that name no operator

A filter step whose name no operator has, such as `[tagg[x]]`, gets the same hint: ~TiddlyWiki silently tests a field of that name instead, so a misspelt operator returns nothing. A step naming a field on purpose stays quiet when some tiddler has a field of that name, or when a shadow tiddler's filter writes it, as the core's table of contents writes `toc-link[no]`. A dotted name is a function, checked as a call. These hints are not listed by the command below.

!! Quick fix

On a flagged name, <kbd>Ctrl+.</kbd> or the light bulb offers up to three existing names spelled closest to it: at most two letters changed, or one for a name of four letters or fewer, where two swapped neighbours count as one. A widget tag is offered widgets, and its closing tag changes with it; a filter step is offered filter operators.

!! List undefined calls and widgets

An editor checks only the files it has open, and the VS Code Problems panel leaves hints out. The command //~TiddlyWiki LSP: List undefined calls and widgets// asks the server to read every `.tid` file of the wiki, and lists the calls and widget tags whose name nothing defines in the Problems panel, as information. A click on an entry opens its file, and the quick fix works there too.

Links to missing tiddlers are not listed: a wiki routinely links to tiddlers it has yet to write, so they are warned about only in an open file. Once listed, the entries stay while you open and close their files, so a flagged name shows a blue squiggle instead of the grey dots until the window reloads.
